Upgrading Symfony
*****************

.[perex]
Since Symfony 6.0, over 650 things have changed in fifty components and bridges: `MASTER_REQUEST` became `MAIN_REQUEST`, attributes of the framework replaced the SensioFrameworkExtraBundle annotations, and the name of a command moved into `#[AsCommand]`. The package `dresscode/upgrade-symfony` describes all of them. DressCode fixes roughly 440 right away and reports the rest, together with what to write instead.

The upgrades from Symfony 6 to [Symfony 7 |upgrade-to-symfony-7] and from Symfony 7.4 to [Symfony 8 |upgrade-to-symfony-8] have pages of their own, with an example and the steps to follow.


Installation
============

```shell
composer require --dev dresscode/upgrade-symfony phpstan/phpstan
vendor/bin/dresscode upgrade today
```

You need no configuration for it: `upgrade today` finds the data and the rules of the package on its own and leaves the style of the code as it is. [Upgrading Code |upgrade] describes how to go one major version at a time and what to do with what is left.

Every check can watch for the old API too. For that, the configuration needs three things: the types, the set `deprecations`, and the name of the package, which enables its rules in the section `symfony`, as described in [How Upgrading Libraries Works |upgrading-libraries#Enabling It in the Configuration]:

```neon
typeAnalysis: phpstan

use:
	- symfony         # your standard
	- deprecations
	- upgrade-symfony
```

The data itself turns on two decisions in the section `symfony`, `symfony.isGrantedAttribute` from `symfony/security-http` 6.2 and `symfony.valueResolver` from `symfony/http-kernel` 6.2, so you do not have to add them to the configuration, and `upgrade today` runs them too. If you do not want one of them, turn it off in the section `symfony` with the value `keep`. The data of each component applies only when the project uses that component, and only for the versions the project has reached. A project that requires the whole `symfony/symfony` has all the components that package replaces, in the version of `symfony/symfony`.


What Gets Fixed
===============

Here are a command and a controller written for Symfony 5.4, in a project that now uses the current versions:

```php .[before]
class ReportCommand extends Command
{
	protected static $defaultName = 'app:report';  // Property `Symfony\Component\Console\Command\Command::$defaultName` is replaced by the attribute `#[Symfony\Component\Console\Attribute\AsCommand]`.
	protected static $defaultDescription = 'Sends the daily report';  // Property `Symfony\Component\Console\Command\Command::$defaultDescription` is replaced by the attribute `#[Symfony\Component\Console\Attribute\AsCommand]`.


	protected function execute(InputInterface $input, OutputInterface $output): int
	{
		$request = $this->requestStack->getMasterRequest();  // Method `Symfony\Component\HttpFoundation\RequestStack::getMasterRequest()` is replaced by `RequestStack::getMainRequest()`.
		$output->writeln($request->get('day'));  // Method `Symfony\Component\HttpFoundation\Request::get()` is forbidden: read the bag the value comes from, the `attributes`, `query` or `request` property, such as `query->get()`.
		return Command::SUCCESS;
	}
}


class ReportController extends AbstractController
{
	/** @Security("is_granted('ROLE_ADMIN')") */  // Annotation `@Security` is replaced by the attribute `#[Symfony\Component\Security\Http\Attribute\IsGranted]`.
	public function show(): Response
	{
		return new Response($this->getUser()->getUsername());  // Method `Symfony\Component\Security\Core\User\UserInterface::getUsername()` is replaced by `getUserIdentifier()`.
	}
}
```

After `dresscode upgrade today`:

```php .[after]
#[AsCommand(name: 'app:report', description: 'Sends the daily report')]
class ReportCommand extends Command
{
	protected function execute(InputInterface $input, OutputInterface $output): int
	{
		$request = $this->requestStack->getMainRequest();
		$output->writeln($request->get('day'));
		return Command::SUCCESS;
	}
}


class ReportController extends AbstractController
{
	#[IsGranted('ROLE_ADMIN')]
	public function show(): Response
	{
		return new Response($this->getUser()->getUserIdentifier());
	}
}
```

The two static properties merged into one attribute, and the expression of the annotation `@Security` became the role of the attribute `#[IsGranted]`, both with their imports. The call `$request->get()` was left alone: `Request::get()` looks for the value in the attributes, in the query and in the body of the request, and you have to decide which of them your code reads it from.

What the package fixes:

- **renamed classes and interfaces**: `Symfony\Component\Security\Core\Security` to `Symfony\Bundle\SecurityBundle\Security`, `TaggedIterator` to `AutowireIterator`, the Sendinblue bridge to Brevo and others; the import, the type, `new`, `instanceof` and the attribute are all rewritten,
- **renamed members**: `getMasterRequest()` to `getMainRequest()`, `HttpKernelInterface::MASTER_REQUEST` to `MAIN_REQUEST`, `getUsername()` to `getUserIdentifier()`, `Application::add()` to `addCommand()`,
- **calls of a different shape**: a parameter that got a different name (`new UriSigner($secret, parameter: ...)` to `hashParameter:`), a key of an options array renamed in place (`mirror()` with `copy_on_windows` to `follow_symlinks`), the container configuration function `ref()` to `service()`,
- **SensioFrameworkExtraBundle annotations** to attributes of the framework: `@Route`, `@Cache`, `@IsGranted` and `@Template`, and `@Security`, whose expression only calls `is_granted()`, to `#[IsGranted]`; routing and serializer annotations from the namespace `Annotation` to attributes from `Attribute`; validator constraint annotations and `@UniqueEntity` to the attributes of the same name, except `@ExpressionLanguageSyntax`, which becomes `#[ExpressionSyntax]`; and `@required` to `#[Required]`,
- **a class member to an attribute**: `$defaultName` and `$defaultDescription` of a command, and an overridden `getDefaultName()` too, to `#[AsCommand]`, and the interface `MessageHandlerInterface` to `#[AsMessageHandler]`,
- **a controller argument resolver** written against `ArgumentValueResolverInterface` to `ValueResolverInterface`: its `supports()` becomes a condition at the start of `resolve()`,
- **signatures** of methods in child classes of framework classes: the return types and parameters a component added (`execute(): int` of a command); the decision `classes.overridingMethod.signature` writes them from the declarations of the installed version, without data. Adding a return type is a risky fix, because the body of the method may return something else, so DressCode makes it only with `--fix-risky`, after your consent in `--ask-risky`, or with the decision in the key [`fixRisky` |configuration#risky-fixes].

The decisions `symfony.isGrantedAttribute` and `symfony.valueResolver` rewrite the annotation `@Security`, also in the form of the attribute `#[Security]` of the same bundle, and the resolver; their rules come with the package. The rules of the DressCode engine do the rest, following the data; the signatures need no data at all.


What Is Left to You
===================

Where there is no replacement, or the replacement is used differently, DressCode reports the place and says what to write. This covers, for example, services a controller used to take from the container, or an interface that should be replaced by attributes on individual methods:

```
Method `Symfony\Bundle\FrameworkBundle\Controller\AbstractController::getDoctrine()` is forbidden: use a constructor or action argument typed with `Doctrine\Persistence\ManagerRegistry`.
Class `Symfony\Component\Messenger\Handler\MessageSubscriberInterface` is forbidden: write the attribute `AsMessageHandler` on each method that handles a message, with what `getHandledMessages()` yields for it as its arguments.
```

What a component only quietly marks as deprecated is not in the data. With the types, DressCode reports it anyway, and where the annotation names the replacement, it rewrites the code through `upgrading.declarations.deprecatedMember`. The data does not capture changes in behavior, in YAML configuration or in Twig templates at all; you will find those in the `UPGRADE-*.md` guides of the Symfony repository.


What the Package Covers
=======================

| component | versions |
|---|---|
| `symfony/asset`, `symfony/asset-mapper` | 6.0 to 7.1 |
| `symfony/browser-kit`, `symfony/dom-crawler` | 6.0 to 7.4 |
| `symfony/cache` | 6.0 to 7.1 |
| `symfony/config` | 6.0 to 7.4 |
| `symfony/console` | 6.0 to 7.4 |
| `symfony/dependency-injection` | 6.0 to 8.1 |
| `symfony/doctrine-bridge` | 6.0 to 8.2 |
| `symfony/event-dispatcher` | 6.0 |
| `symfony/expression-language` | 7.1 |
| `symfony/filesystem`, `symfony/finder` | 6.0 to 8.2 |
| `symfony/form` | 6.0 to 8.1 |
| `symfony/framework-bundle` | 6.0 to 7.4 |
| `symfony/html-sanitizer` | 7.4 |
| `symfony/http-client` | 6.0 to 8.1 |
| `symfony/http-foundation` | 6.0 to 8.1 |
| `symfony/http-kernel` | 6.0 to 8.2 |
| `symfony/intl` | 6.0 |
| `symfony/json-streamer`, `symfony/type-info` | 7.2 to 8.1 |
| `symfony/lock` | 6.0 to 6.3 |
| `symfony/mailer`, `symfony/mime`, `symfony/notifier` | 6.0 to 7.4 |
| `symfony/messenger` | 6.0 to 6.4 |
| `symfony/monolog-bridge` | 6.0 to 7.4 |
| `symfony/options-resolver` | 6.0 |
| `symfony/property-access`, `symfony/property-info` | 6.0 to 7.3 |
| `symfony/routing` | 6.0 to 7.4 |
| `symfony/security-bundle`, `symfony/security-core`, `symfony/security-csrf` | 6.0 to 7.4 |
| `symfony/security-http` | 6.0 to 8.1 |
| `symfony/serializer` | 6.0 to 7.4 |
| `symfony/string` | 7.4 |
| `symfony/translation` | 6.2 to 7.4 |
| `symfony/twig-bridge` | 6.2 to 7.2 |
| `symfony/validator` | 6.0 to 8.2 |
| `symfony/var-dumper`, `symfony/var-exporter` | 6.2 to 7.3 |
| `symfony/workflow` | 6.0 to 7.3 |
| bridges `brevo-mailer`, `brevo-notifier`, `mail-pace-mailer`, `sevenio-notifier`, `google-chat-notifier`, `slack-notifier` | 6.0 to 7.3 |

The versions show the releases in which a component changed something the data captures. The data of a retired bridge is listed under the bridge that replaced it: the rename of Sendinblue to Brevo applies in a project that already has `symfony/brevo-mailer`. An application on Symfony 5.4 is upgraded the same way, with version 6.0 as the first step; [Upgrading Code |upgrade#One Major Version at a Time, by Hand] describes how to proceed one major version at a time.

